# Format (the pack signature)

> Purpose: every rule and every AI-written project file looks and behaves the same, whichever AI writes it.

## 1. The flow (every decision, every time)

**Ask → Recommend → Decide → Write → Confirm**

| Step | What the AI does |
|------|------------------|
| **Ask** | Ask the dev in plain language; one short batch per topic. The dev may answer, skip, or say **"you decide"** |
| **Recommend** | Propose options as a **Recommendation Block** (below) — exactly **one Recommended**, others as alternatives with trade-offs |
| **Decide** | Dev accepts, changes, or delegates. "You decide" = the Recommended option, recorded as AI-chosen |
| **Write** | Immediately write the decision into its file (never chat-only), with status and date |
| **Confirm** | Show a 2–4 line summary of what was written and where |

Applies to: stack, design direction, layout strategy, per-route layouts, phase plan, scope, anything the dev is unsure about.

## 2. Recommendation Block (how the AI proposes)

Use this exact shape in chat **and** when recording it in a file:

```
### REC-<area>-<n> — <decision title>
- Question:     <what is being decided>
- Recommended:  <option> — <why it fits THIS concept, max 3 bullets>
- Alternatives: <option B> (<trade-off>) · <option C> (<trade-off>)
- Decision:     pending | accepted | changed to <option> | AI-chosen — <who>, <date>
- Written to:   <file → section>
```

`<area>` ∈ `stack` · `design` · `layout` · `route` · `scope` · `data` · `plan`. Max 3 options. Always one Recommended.

## 3. Rule file anatomy (pack rules in `.cursor/rules/`)

```
---
description: <one line>
alwaysApply: true | false
globs: <only when scoped>
---
# <Title>
> Purpose: <one line>

## Rules          ← numbered or table; imperative; no repeats of other files
## Flow           ← only if the rule has steps
## Do not         ← only bans specific to THIS file (global bans live in restrictions.mdc)
```

- One topic per file. **No basic/advanced split** — everything a topic needs is in its one file; optional parts are marked *(when the concept needs it)*.
- Say a thing **once**, in the file that owns it; elsewhere link by filename.

## 4. Project file anatomy (AI-filled files: `concept-domain.mdc`, `route-layouts.mdc`, `phase-plan.mdc`, `frontend-design/SKILL.md`; the P1 deliverables in `docs/` carry the same `Status:` line)

```
# <Title>
> Status: TEMPLATE | DRAFT | APPROVED — filled by <AI/tool>, <date>; approved by <dev|—>

## Decisions      ← Recommendation Blocks (accepted ones)
## Content        ← the actual spec, using the ID scheme below
## Change log     ← one line per change: date · AI · what · why
```

- `TEMPLATE` → nothing filled. `DRAFT` → AI filled, awaiting dev. `APPROVED` → dev accepted; changes need dev ask + log line.
- Edit the file **first**, then the code. Bump status back to `DRAFT` when a change needs re-approval.

## 5. ID scheme (use everywhere)

| ID | Meaning | Example |
|----|---------|---------|
| `F-nn` | Feature | `F-03` Checkout |
| `R-nn` | Route/page | `R-07` `/orders/:id` |
| `E-nn` | API endpoint | `E-12` `POST /orders` |
| `T-nn` | Table/entity | `T-02` `orders` |
| `U-nn` | Function / unit / component (plan item) | `U-041` `OrderService.create()` |
| `REC-<area>-<n>` | Recommendation | `REC-layout-1` |

Plan items reference these IDs (`U-041 → F-03, E-12, T-02`). No item without a feature.

## 6. Status words (only these)

Plan items: `todo` · `doing` · `done` · `deferred (reason)`. Files: `TEMPLATE` · `DRAFT` · `APPROVED`. Decisions: `pending` · `accepted` · `changed` · `AI-chosen`.

## 7. Reading & size

Rules are `alwaysApply: false` except `format`, `restrictions`, `multi-ai`; detail lives in `.cursor/reference/` and is read on demand (`CURSOR.md` §6 Load map). Keep project files scannable by ID so an AI can read one block, not the file.

## 8. Writing style

Imperative, present tense, concrete (name regions, functions, values). Tables for options and specs; short bullets for rules. No filler, no "should probably".
